---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
const title = "Maximising usability: The Principles of Universal Design";
---
<BaseLayout title={title}>
  <h1>{title}</h1>
  <p class="meta">My take on the paper.</p>

  <div class="paper">
    <p>
      Although the paper effectively explains universal design and the full range of impairments it should address to be considered truly universal, I think 'universal design' and 'accessible design' should be synonyms, rather than treating 'universal design' as a subset of 'accessible design'.
    </p>

    <p>
      I agree with the author’s claim that adding accessibility features can be expensive and requires users to accommodate the feature rather than having it work for them. Universal design eliminates the need for these “additional” features while still leaving room for adaptable design, which can be expensive.
    </p>

    <p>
      In my opinion, adaptable and transgenerational design should be considered modifications of universal design, rather than treating universal, adaptable, and transgenerational design as three distinct concepts that intersect at some points.
    </p>

    <p>
      According to the author, transgenerational design accounts for age-acquired impairments but does not account for factors like gender, culture, and literacy level that are missing in the transgenerational design.
    </p>

    <figure class="figure">
      <img src="/figures/reading-1/figure2.png" alt="Decision tree about disease vs disability framing" />
    </figure>

    <p>
      The above diagram, in my opinion, could better account for the missing factors in the transgenerational design. It could also reduce the cost of adaptable design by reducing the level of customisation needed for an individual.
    </p>

    <p>
      The Principle of Universal Design seems to be robust and inclusive of a range of disabilities without naming the impairment, which is a good point, as naming the impairment could isolate the principle to one impairment. For example, the captioning and transcript are useful for people on the spectrum of auditory impairment, as well as people with cognitive and developmental impairment.
    </p>

    <p>
      I do want to highlight that, along with the principle, as there are strategies to guide how the principles should be implemented, there are tests that should be passed. I believe there should be a standard guideline for tests if a solution is marketed to a particular impairment, and it should be coupled with in-depth customer discovery.
    </p>

    <p style="text-align: center;">
      Citation:
    </p>

    <p>
      Molly Follette Story M.S. (1998) Maximizing Usability: The Principles of Universal Design, Assistive Technology, 10:1, 4–12, DOI: 10.1080/10400435.1998.10131955
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
